WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.211
Common crypto review findings in .

2
00:00:02.211 --> 00:00:05.853
NET applications are often practical rather than exotic.

3
00:00:05.853 --> 00:00:19.770
Teams may find custom algorithms, copied snippets whose assumptions are not understood, hardcoded keys, shared keys across environments, or low-level primitives used where a framework service would have been safer.

4
00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.567
Password storage is a frequent review area.

5
00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:30.046
Fast hashes are useful in some integrity contexts, but they are not appropriate for password storage by themselves.

6
00:00:30.046 --> 00:00:38.695
Password-based systems should use maintained password hashing behavior with salts and work factors rather than custom verifier logic.

7
00:00:38.695 --> 00:00:42.987
Encryption without integrity protection is another common problem.

8
00:00:42.987 --> 00:00:46.239
Confidentiality alone may not detect modification.

9
00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:56.904
Authenticated encryption or a reviewed framework protection service is usually a better fit when the application must know that protected data has not been changed.

10
00:00:56.904 --> 00:01:01.392
Nonce or IV handling should be reviewed against the chosen algorithm.

11
00:01:01.392 --> 00:01:11.797
Weak random generation, repeated values where uniqueness is required, or improvised construction from timestamps and counters can undermine the intended design.

12
00:01:11.797 --> 00:01:16.545
Randomness and uniqueness rules are part of the protocol, not decoration.

13
00:01:16.545 --> 00:01:21.032
Certificate validation should not be disabled in production behavior.

14
00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:25.714
Accepting any certificate removes important service identity guarantees.

15
00:01:25.714 --> 00:01:34.364
If a development environment needs a special trust path, that path should be environment-scoped and not become a production shortcut.

16
00:01:34.364 --> 00:01:37.615
Secrets can also leak through ordinary operations.

17
00:01:37.615 --> 00:01:47.240
Logs, URLs, screenshots, source control, support tickets, build output, and client-delivered assets can all expose keys or decrypted sensitive data.

18
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:54.264
Safe crypto review includes redaction, operational workflows, and deployment behavior, not only source code.

19
00:01:54.264 --> 00:02:11.043
A safe review process starts with the security property needed, the protected data, the key owner, the storage location, the maintained API or framework service, rotation expectations, incident response expectations, and documented assumptions or exceptions.
